  wanted it this way she has this  she has this will see that says that  that we have to do it this way  we both do  all the actors and there were some  really wonderful actors there to work  with  but shailene you know she was a  professional  but she was more than a professional  when she walked onto that set  i think one of the first scenes we shot  i think we're letting her go because  when i tell her  her mom's not going to make it and she  dives underwater  she cries underwater and i've never seen  anything like in my life  and i said to her then i go look i don't  know what's going to happen with this  film but you  don't worry about your career it's funny  over the life of  our lives now you know we write each  other and  i really do consider her like my eldest  daughter you know  she is a dear someone i dearly love  i was on my way to swim in the black  point pool with brandy  and suddenly i see mom and some  douchebag walking into a house  his house i guess just some guy it could  be anybody alexander payne i think is a  brilliant brilliant director and  his understanding of what i needed to be  and it was you know fascinating because  i  i would my my persona is  to push against being the guy that gets  a pie in the face  i always figure i'd like to do any part  but i don't want to be the guy that gets  a  you know somebody throws a pie in your  face and then everybody laughs at you  and the only way that the film works is  if for the first  two-thirds of the film i'm the guy that  gets the pie in his face and  you have to let it happen you just have  to there's no other options  and so it was not a comfortable acting  job for me  in that way because you know everything  is like everybody  you know everybody who comes in the room  is smarter than me and everybody's  got their act together better than i do  so it was a trickier one  but i also knew that i was in such good  hands  alexander hadn't had a movie not be  successful  you know not be critically successful  and the script was terrific  i knew my part was great by the end i  knew the scene where he says  goodbye to his wife was  his sort of moment of strength and his  moment you know when  i knew that the story had a circle to it  and that circle would be  eventually joined and i knew that the  family being back together was gonna  work  again another sort of tableau shot with  everybody just sitting there  uh you know watching television together  but i felt like  in the hands of alexander i didn't i  wasn't worried about it at all  midnight sky i knew  that ultimately it was a story about  redemption  and it was a story about it was a much  smaller story  i have to warn them about the conditions  on earth  you know i wanted it to be like a silent  movie in some of it i wanted it to be  a meditation in a way of loss  we weren't in the middle of a pandemic  when it happened but there were still  all these other elements  you know these elements of how much  hate and anger were sort of all of us  experiencing at this moment in our  history all over the world you know go  to  bolsonaro in brazil or orban and hungary  or  look around lots of anger and hate  and that if you play together this takes  place in 2049 if you played it out  you know 30 years this could very well  be what our reality is if that kind of  hate is allowed to  to fester i knew that the idea was that  we were taking  these big canvases and we're telling  very small stories  small stories i can tell i know how to  do the big canvases  i needed help you know and that was with  this incredible cinematographer  uh martin roo and with these great the  effects guys and they really helped us  and then you know it's easy when you go  out in  iceland on 70 mile an hour wind it's 40  degrees below zero  it doesn't take a whole lot of acting to  be cold  you know or be in pain you want to be an  explorer  but while you're doing all that your own  life is just  slipping away that's why i have to  contact them  ilm came in with these huge led  backdrops  that we used that had cameras facing our  camera  so that the backdrops for just for the  winter stuff outside  when we're in the snow so that when  the camera moved the the the camera  shooting the camera would know and would  change the backdrop to the angle of  where we were moving to really amazing  stuff and it was a technical nightmare  as you can imagine  but you know we finished ahead of  schedule and under budget we did  everything we were supposed to do  you know it's about the idea of first  and foremost what man could do to man  if we don't pay attention what we are  capable of doing  but it's also about the spirit of who we  really are  you know if you boil us down and that  there's this  need for redemption and this need  to be good and kind and fair  and all of those things that as as the  film comes to its climax there's all of  these judgments that are made the two  guys who decide to go home  there's a judgment made there i owe to  my wife or  i want to take this girl home you know  there are these things that are you know  life and death decisions but they're  they show such great  human character that i i i just love the  script because of that i love the idea  of the story  also you know we watched it a couple  days ago you know unfortunately for a  lot of people i don't get to see it on a  giant screen we shot on 65 so we watched  it a couple days ago in westwood at the  village  on this big screen and you know  it's just stunning the the the shots  that those that  that they put together the even the the  spaceship stuff  out in the space of spacewalk when you  see it you know in 65 it's really  something  so in gravity you worked with director  alfonso quaron did you take what you  learned with him  and apply it to this one the thing about  gravity alfonso was this  genius we were doing scenes in gravity  that they hadn't yet  invented the technology for i had like  the wildest experiences of like they'd  put a vr  you know headset on and then i'd hold  another  you know a camera in my hand and in the  vr i'd be walking on the spaceship that  doesn't exist it's all  in vr inside this empty gymnasium  and there'd be a big monitor behind  there with the dp watching it  and i would walk through all the  crevices on the outside of the for the  space walk  and i'd look up there and you could see  the antenna there i go okay this is the  angle this is the shot for here  they'd mark it down and i'd walk around  so i was able to actually  shoot as if i was on this giant thing  that we built when we were only going to  build  pieces of it and the rest was going to  be cgi  amazing experience and a great lesson my  goal in my career  was to always say look i and i just i  would say it constantly  uh and luckily my agents were kind about  that which i just said look  my job is you know i i don't want to  worry about  i don't want to just be able at the end  of my career to say how many films open  the number one  you know i don't care about the opening  weekend i wanted them to be  films that would hold up longer than an  opening weekend and  i've been lucky enough to have a few and  you know that  that makes me feel very i'm very i feel  very lucky
 

